      "writtenOn": "2025-04-07T17:06:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve taken another look at `java_device_for_host` and I\u0027ve realised I originally misunderstood how it worked. I\u0027ve had a go at implementing a `cc_device_for_host` module however I\u0027ve gotten a bit stuck with how the dependency tags, module providers and `PathDeps` work together to form the input and output of a module. Presumably adding a dependency (of the correct tag) from a `cc_device_for_host` module to a `cc_object` module should allow soong to pass the `.o` output through the `cc_device_for_host` module to whatever is including it as an input?\n\nI\u0027ve taken a look at the tags and it looks like the `reuseObjTag` or `objDepTag` could be used for this purpose as they both set `depPaths.Objs`. Would you agree?\n\nFinally, do you have a reason for preferring this method over the `force_arm64` property? As far as I can tell, the `force_arm64` property addresses the issue with keeping the toolchain flags in line with regular Arm64 flags. I\u0027m not sure the `cc_device_for_host` method is providing any additional benefits while being much more complex logically.\n\nNote: the only files that can be used with `force_arm64` are simple Arm64 assembly files which do not vary between host and target variants. As long as the correct toolchain is selected, these files should compile identically regardless of variant.",
      "parentUuid": "f5b7879f_6b1890a4",
      "revId": "99fd863014bffe145d795858c4a5c01a5d44e47c",
